Say which instruction wrote a watched address, and optionally only when it
carries a chosen value.

LSW2_WRITE_WATCH=<addr> reports the pc, lr and low registers for every guest
store to that address. unmapped() already proved this seam can name an
instruction; this asks it the same question about a chosen address.

LSW2_WRITE_WATCH_VALUE=<word> narrows it to stores carrying that exact value.
Without it the watch cannot be aimed at an I/O port: the geometry engine's
GXFIFO takes several thousand stores per frame, so the report cap is spent
inside the first frame. With it, a polygon's TEXIMAGE_PARAM is a distinctive
enough word to name the routine that submits that one polygon - which is how
the top-screen portrait's drawing code was found.

The value filter lives HERE rather than in a patch of its own because it has
to insert a line inside the function this patch adds; as a separate patch the
two could no longer be applied or reversed independently.

The call-chain print (LSW2_WRITE_WATCH_STACK=1) lives here for the same
reason: it prints from inside this function. For a store inside a shared
helper - the MT19937 draw at 0x02062D10 - lr only names the helper's caller;
the runtime's own record of pushed guest return addresses names the chain.

